  "image_name": "3cfee4fc56bb5e202a2ac1d806ae019f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes strategies for improving organic afterglow materials for bioimaging and related applications. Overall, it shows how brightness, wavelength, signal specificity, and tissue penetration can all be tuned through molecular design and nanoparticle engineering.\n\nA major theme is boosting afterglow brightness by increasing reactive oxygen species generation, improving chemiexcitation, and making energy transfer more efficient. The schemes compare different sensitizers and donor–acceptor systems, showing that intermolecular or intramolecular transfer can shift emission from weak green/yellow afterglow toward brighter red or near‑infrared output. Another route combines aggregation-caused quenching and aggregation-induced emission effects to improve energy use and greatly enhance brightness.\n\nThe figure also highlights ways to extend emission wavelength. Co-assembly with relay units produces a red shift, while molecular engineering—especially extending conjugation or introducing heteroatoms—pushes emission from shorter visible wavelengths toward deeper red and near‑infrared regions.\n\nFor signal specificity, the image depicts activatable probes that remain “off” until triggered by biomarkers or biological conditions such as enzymes, pH, or oxidants, which then turn afterglow “on.” Finally, it compares excitation methods, suggesting that ultrasound or X‑rays can enable deeper tissue penetration than ordinary light, improving in vivo afterglow imaging.",
